    rule2Text: "Peracetic acid and its equilibrium hydrogen peroxide aggressively attack the unsaturated double bonds of Nitrile (NBR) elastomers, inducing rapid crosslink degradation, severe Shore A hardening (>20 points), micro-cracking, and flange blowout within weeks. Standardize on FKM (Viton®) or expanded PTFE for all chemical injection manifold gaskets and pipe couplings.",
    rule3Title: "3. Maintain Continuous Exhaust Ventilation to Satisfy OSHA 0.4 ppm PAA STEL Limits",
    rule3Text: "Peracetic acid exhibits significant vapor-liquid volatility. At operating wash temperatures above 50°C, airborne PAA vapor concentrates rapidly in the washer headspace. Without an interlocked, dedicated mechanical scrubber or forced-draft flue pulling at least 1,200 CFM, pungent peracid vapors will spill into the dishroom, breaching OSHA and ACGIH Short-Term Exposure Limits (STEL of 0.4 ppm / 1.24 mg/m³) and triggering acute eye and airway irritation.",
    rule4Title: "4. Control Water Chlorides and Maintain Sanitizer pH Between 4.0 and 4.8",
    rule4Text: "Unbuffered peracetic acid concentrates can drop wash water pH below 3.5. Acidic pH accelerates both metal dissolution and hydrogen embrittlement. Simultaneously, incoming municipal water with high chloride levels (>100 mg/L) forms a lethal synergy with peracids. Install reverse osmosis or dealkalizing pre-treatment if municipal chloride exceeds 80 ppm, and calibrate dosing pumps daily to prevent excessive chemical spikes.",
